page Benchmarks "/benchmarks" {
  meta title="Benchmarks · ArtScript" description="What it costs Claude to build and change the same apps in ArtScript, React, Next.js, Svelte, Vue and SolidJS."
  state detail = false

  column class="container page-pad" gap=8 {
    column gap=3 {
      text "Benchmarks" class="crumb"
      title "What an app costs to build with AI" class="page-title" tag=h1
      text "Claude built the same apps in each stack: small apps from scratch, full-stack apps, and changes to existing projects of 11, 42 and 102 components. Every app was run and used in a simulated browser. The cost is the real API bill: the spec in the prompt, every retry and the thinking tokens." class="page-lead"
    }
    grid cols=1 lg:cols=3 gap=5 {
      for m in RESULTS {
        card class="model" gap=4 {
          column gap=1 {
            text m.name bold
            text `−${m.headline}%` class="big-pct grad"
            text "cost per working app vs React + TypeScript" muted small
          }
          for r in m.rows {
            column gap=1 {
              row justify=between {
                text r.stack bold=r.ours small
                text `$${r.usd.toFixed(4)} · ${r.solved}` class="bar-usd" small
              }
              column class="bar-track" style="height:12px" {
                column class=(r.ours ? "bar-fill ours" : "bar-fill") style=`width:${barWidth(m.rows, r.usd)}`
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    text "Next.js was measured on 2026-11-04, with the three models, and so was ArtScript's Sonnet card. The other cells are from 2026-10-02." muted small
    card class="callout" gap=2 {
      text "Read the numbers honestly" bold
      text "Two or three runs per task is an early signal, not a definitive benchmark. With the smallest model, Haiku, ArtScript solved fewer of the new-app tasks than React and Next.js (39/48, against 40/48 and 44/48). The raw data of every run is in the repository, and anyone can reproduce it with npm run eval."
    }
    card class="callout" gap=3 {
      text "Where it doesn't save: imperative code" bold
      text "One task where almost all the code is a physics loop on a canvas (gravity, walls, elastic collisions, drawing): there ArtScript is JavaScript with another syntax for statements, and the question is only whether it costs the same. Measured apart from the tables above, two runs per cell, USD per solved task." class="body"
      table {
        tr {
          th "Model"
          for c in IMPERATIVE[0].cells {
            th c.stack
          }
          th "vs React"
        }
        for m in IMPERATIVE key m.model {
          tr {
            td m.model
            for c in m.cells {
              td c.usd bold=c.ours
            }
            td m.vsReact bold
          }
        }
      }
      text "Four runs per cell (two full rounds), with the full spec and no project rules: here ArtScript cost more than React with every model. The breakdown showed why: the code was still a quarter shorter, but the model deliberated 650–1,600 extra tokens writing a less familiar syntax, and retried more. Then, measured apart (ArtScript only, two runs per model): with the project rule that puts the physics in a .ts file imported with use, and the core spec with a whole app as the example, every model wrote sim.ts + app.art at the first attempt with React-level thinking: Sonnet $0.0227 (React $0.0209), Opus $0.0618 (React $0.0634), Haiku $0.0084 (React $0.0110). New projects now carry that rule and the core spec. Then the core spec was measured on all 50 tasks (ArtScript cells, two runs): Sonnet $0.0092 against $0.0088 with the full spec, Haiku $0.0088 against $0.0088, Opus $0.0239 against $0.0220: the same results, no cheaper, so the full spec stays what new projects read first." class="body"
    }
    card class="callout" gap=3 {
      text "Against Next.js, with and without a backend" bold
      text "Per working result, ArtScript cost 50% less than Next.js 16 with Sonnet, 45% less with Opus and 29% less with Haiku. Next.js costs about the same as React with Sonnet and Opus, also on the full-stack tasks: writing route handlers wasn't cheaper for the model than a hand-written Node server."
      text "With Haiku, Next.js solved more tasks than ArtScript: 96/100 against 90/100, and 16/18 against 13/18 on the full-stack ones. It costs more per result, but it fails less with the smallest model. For real work, use Sonnet or Opus: both stacks solved every task with them." bold
      table {
        tr {
          th "Model"
          th "USD per working result"
          th "ArtScript"
          th "Next.js"
          th "Difference"
          th "React"
        }
        for r in NEXT_ROWS key `${r.model} ${r.tasks}` {
          tr {
            td r.model
            td r.tasks
            td r.art bold
            td r.next
            td r.diff bold
            td r.react
          }
        }
      }
      text "How it was measured: every Next.js answer had to pass a real next build, a real next start served its route handlers, and the pages went through the same checks as every other stack, in a simulated browser with a small stand-in for Next's client router. Its apps ship about 147 KB of JavaScript (brotli, all of the build's client chunks), against about 8 KB."
      text "Keep in mind: Next.js and ArtScript's Sonnet cell are from 2026-11-04; ArtScript with Opus and Haiku and React are from 2026-10-02, when ArtScript's spec was shorter (with Sonnet that made it about 5% more expensive). Two runs per task. The Next.js apps use route handlers and client components, with data in memory; Server Actions, and Server Components reading data directly, weren't measured." muted small
      row gap=2 wrap {
        link "Sonnet raw data" href="https://github.com/leb90/ArtScript/blob/main/benchmarks/eval/results/2026-11-04T16-50-49-claude-sonnet-5-5.md" class="pill"
        link "Opus raw data" href="https://github.com/leb90/ArtScript/blob/main/benchmarks/eval/results/2026-11-04T18-03-56-claude-opus-5-5.md" class="pill"
        link "Haiku raw data" href="https://github.com/leb90/ArtScript/blob/main/benchmarks/eval/results/2026-11-04T17-56-39-claude-haiku-4-5.md" class="pill"
      }
    }
    card class="callout" gap=3 {
      text "Motion-heavy design: measured too" bold
      text "A landing with a hero that rises in, a gradient that drifts behind it, three cards that reveal on scroll one after another, a button that grows on hover and a number that counts up. In ArtScript those are flags on any element (reveal, animate=, stagger, hover=); in the other stacks the model writes the CSS, an IntersectionObserver and a requestAnimationFrame loop. The code ArtScript writes is 40–55% shorter and Sonnet and Opus solve it at the first attempt; measured per fresh conversation it still costs more (the spec written to the cache each time, plus 900–1,200 tokens of thinking over props the model hasn't seen), and in a session with the spec cached the same runs cost 3–9% less than React. The table and the full reading are on the benchmarks section of the README." class="body"
    }
    card class="callout" gap=2 {
      text "Runtime speed" bold
      text "In the official js-framework-benchmark results (Chrome 154, October 2026), ArtScript scores 1.32 (1.00 is the fastest keyed implementation): between Svelte (1.24) and Vue (1.33), 1.14× Solid and well ahead of React (1.91), with 7.5 KB compressed. It ties the fastest at clearing rows and is slowest relative to Solid at creating 10,000 rows (1.20×)."
      row gap=2 wrap {
        link "The official table" href="https://krausest.github.io/js-framework-benchmark/2026/chrome154.html" class="pill"
        link "Per-operation numbers and how to reproduce" href="https://github.com/leb90/ArtScript/blob/main/benchmarks/js-framework-benchmark/RESULTS.md" class="pill"
      }
    }
    column gap=3 {
      title "How it was measured" class="doc-title"
      Markdown html=methodologyHtml
    }
    column gap=3 align=start {
      button (detail ? "Hide the full tables" : "Show every table, per task and per project") class="ghost" -> detail = !detail
      if detail {
        Markdown html=resultsHtml
      }
    }
  }
}
